{{> judge-opening}}

JUDGMENT POINT: list EVERY NOUN of the adopted Thesis and of each Strand claim in the input -- `thesis` and `strand_claims` -- and classify each `defined` or `ordinary`. A term is one or more words exactly as they occur in that text, in the form an `introduces` entry's `term` takes (so `non-member fallback` is one term). `defined` means this article uses the word in a sense it gives it -- a CI-specific or article-specific sense the reader does not already hold -- EVEN WHEN THE WORD IS EVERYDAY ENGLISH: `check`, `guard`, `list` or `pass` used in a CI sense is `defined`. `ordinary` means the reader already holds the sense the article uses. The test is on the sense, never on whether the word is common.
REQUIRED RECORD SHAPE: ONE record AT ITS OWN ROOT -- `version` "1" and `defined_terms`, a list of `{ term, class }` entries, as src/candidate-schema.json's `defined_terms_record` declares
WHAT IS REFUSED: a record whose `version` is not "1"; `defined_terms` not a list; an entry that is not a `{ term, class }` map, carries any other key, carries no term, or a class outside `defined`, `ordinary`; a term listed twice; a term that occurs whole-word in neither the Thesis nor any Strand claim. The next Call receives the refused record beside its refusal, both verbatim (kogaki#1438).

